<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>circular-motion</title></head><body><h1>circular-motion</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Angular Displacement, Velocity and Acceleration</li><li>Angular Displacement, Velocity and Acceleration</li><li>Centripetal Acceleration and Force</li><li>Relation Between Angular and Linear Velocity and Acceleration</li><li>Centripetal Acceleration and Centripetal Force</li><li>Conical Pendulum</li><li>Motion in a Vertical Circle</li><li>Applications of Banking</li><li>Motion in a Vertical Circle</li><li>Applications of Banking</li></ul><h2>Angular Displacement, Velocity and Acceleration</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Angular displacement ($\Delta \theta$):** the angle through which a body rotates about a fixed axis. Measured in radians (rad). 1 rad = 57.3°, 2$\pi$ rad = 360°. Angular displacement is a vector along the axis (right-hand rule): positive for anticlockwise rotation.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Angular velocity ($\omega$):** the rate of change of angular displacement: $\omega = d\theta/dt$ (instantaneous), $\omega = \Delta\theta/t$ (average). Unit: rad/s. Direction: along the axis (right-hand rule). For uniform circular motion, $\omega$ is constant.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Angular acceleration ($\alpha$):** the rate of change of angular velocity: $\alpha = d\omega/dt = d^2\theta/dt^2$. Unit: rad/s$^2$. When $\alpha$ is constant, the kinematic equations mirror linear motion.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Relationship between linear and angular quantities (for circular motion):** $v = r\omega$ (linear speed = radius $\times$ angular speed), $a_t = r\alpha$ (tangential acceleration), $a_c = v^2/r = r\omega^2$ (centripetal acceleration). These links connect rotational and translational motion.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Kinematic equations for constant angular acceleration (mirror linear $v = u + at$, etc.):** $\omega = \omega_0 + \alpha t$; $\theta = \omega_0t + \frac{1}{2}\alpha t^2$; $\omega^2 = \omega_0^2 + 2\alpha\theta$; $\theta = (\omega + \omega_0)t/2$. The structure is identical to linear motion — just swap $x ightarrow \theta$, $v ightarrow \omega$, $a ightarrow \alpha$.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — spinning wheel:** a wheel starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 2 rad/s$^2$ for 5 s. Angular displacement: $\theta = 0 + \frac{1}{2} \times 2 \times 25 = 25$ rad. Final angular velocity: $\omega = 0 + 2 \times 5 = 10$ rad/s. Number of revolutions: $25/(2\pi) \approx 3.98$ rev.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Worked example — decelerating fan:** a fan blade rotating at 120 rpm ($120 \times 2\pi/60 = 12.57$ rad/s) comes to rest in 8 s. Angular acceleration: $\alpha = (0 - 12.57)/8 = -1.57$ rad/s$^2$. Angular displacement: $\theta = 12.57 \times 8/2 = 50.27$ rad $\approx 8$ rev.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** converting between rpm and rad/s; applying the kinematic equations for constant angular acceleration; linking linear and angular quantities ($v = r\omega$, $a = r\omega^2$); finding number of revolutions from angular displacement.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Right-hand rule for angular quantities:** curl the fingers of your right hand in the direction of rotation; your thumb points along the angular velocity/acceleration vector. For anticlockwise rotation (as viewed from above), the vector points upward; for clockwise, it points downward.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Angular displacement is NOT a simple angle:** while measured in radians, angular displacement has direction (along the axis). For rotations greater than 2$\pi$, the magnitude keeps increasing — unlike a simple angle which resets after 360°. This is why angular displacement is a true vector quantity.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Non-uniform circular motion:** when angular acceleration $\alpha \neq 0$, both tangential acceleration ($a_t = r\alpha$) and centripetal acceleration ($a_c = r\omega^2$) are present. The total acceleration is the vector sum: $a = \sqrt{a_t^2 + a_c^2}$. The direction of total acceleration is at angle $\phi = \arctan(a_c/a_t)$ from the tangential direction.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Small angle approximation:** for oscillatory motion (pendulums, springs), when $\theta$ is small ($\theta < 0.1$ rad $\approx 6^\circ$), we can approximate $\sin \theta \approx \theta$ and $\cos \theta \approx 1$. This linearizes the equations and makes them solvable analytically.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Angular quantities as vectors — addition is NOT commutative for large angles:** while infinitesimal angular displacements $d\theta$ are vectors (they commute), finite angular displacements do NOT commute. Rotating 90° about x then 90° about y gives a different result than rotating 90° about y then 90° about x. This is why angular velocity is a true vector but finite angular displacement is not.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Angular displacement is measured in radians; 2π rad = 360° = 1 revolution.</td></tr><tr><td>Angular velocity is a vector along the axis (right-hand rule), not tangential.</td></tr><tr><td>The kinematic equations for rotation mirror linear motion: x→θ, v→ω, a→α.</td></tr><tr><td>v = rω links linear and angular speed — the basis of all circular motion problems.</td></tr><tr><td>Always convert rpm to rad/s before using formulas: multiply by 2π/60.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Angular displacement and angle are the same.' ✅ Angular displacement is a vector (along the axis), while angle is a scalar. For a full revolution, angular displacement = 2π rad, but the body returns to its starting position.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Angular velocity is always constant in circular motion.' ✅ Only in UNIFORM circular motion is ω constant. In non-uniform circular motion, ω changes and there is angular acceleration.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The direction of angular velocity is tangential.' ❌ The direction of angular velocity is ALONG THE AXIS of rotation (right-hand rule), not tangential. Tangential velocity is v = rω, but ω itself points along the axis.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Angular acceleration means the body is speeding up.' ✅ Angular acceleration is the rate of change of angular velocity. If α and ω have the same sign, the body speeds up; if opposite signs, it slows down.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The kinematic equations for rotation are different from linear motion.' ✅ They are IDENTICAL in form: just replace x with θ, v with ω, and a with α. The same problem-solving approach works.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'rpm and rad/s are the same.' ✅ 1 rpm = 2π/60 rad/s ≈ 0.105 rad/s. Always convert rpm to rad/s before using formulas.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Angular displacement (θ), angular velocity (ω = dθ/dt), and angular acceleration (α = dω/dt) describe rotational motion. For constant angular acceleration, the kinematic equations mirror linear motion. The links v = rω and a_c = rω² connect rotational and translational quantities. rpm is converted to rad/s by multiplying by 2π/60.</p><hr><h2>Angular Displacement, Velocity and Acceleration</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Angular displacement ($\Delta \theta$):** the angle through which a body rotates about a fixed axis. Measured in radians (rad). 1 rad = 57.3°, 2$\pi$ rad = 360°. Angular displacement is a vector along the axis (right-hand rule): positive for anticlockwise rotation.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Angular velocity ($\omega$):** the rate of change of angular displacement: $\omega = d\theta/dt$ (instantaneous), $\omega = \Delta\theta/t$ (average). Unit: rad/s. Direction: along the axis (right-hand rule). For uniform circular motion, $\omega$ is constant.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Angular acceleration ($\alpha$):** the rate of change of angular velocity: $\alpha = d\omega/dt = d^2\theta/dt^2$. Unit: rad/s$^2$. When $\alpha$ is constant, the kinematic equations mirror linear motion.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Relationship between linear and angular quantities (for circular motion):** $v = r\omega$ (linear speed = radius $\times$ angular speed), $a_t = r\alpha$ (tangential acceleration), $a_c = v^2/r = r\omega^2$ (centripetal acceleration). These links connect rotational and translational motion.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Kinematic equations for constant angular acceleration (mirror linear $v = u + at$, etc.):** $\omega = \omega_0 + \alpha t$; $\theta = \omega_0t + \frac{1}{2}\alpha t^2$; $\omega^2 = \omega_0^2 + 2\alpha\theta$; $\theta = (\omega + \omega_0)t/2$. The structure is identical to linear motion — just swap $x ightarrow \theta$, $v ightarrow \omega$, $a ightarrow \alpha$.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — spinning wheel:** a wheel starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 2 rad/s$^2$ for 5 s. Angular displacement: $\theta = 0 + \frac{1}{2} \times 2 \times 25 = 25$ rad. Final angular velocity: $\omega = 0 + 2 \times 5 = 10$ rad/s. Number of revolutions: $25/(2\pi) \approx 3.98$ rev.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Worked example — decelerating fan:** a fan blade rotating at 120 rpm ($120 \times 2\pi/60 = 12.57$ rad/s) comes to rest in 8 s. Angular acceleration: $\alpha = (0 - 12.57)/8 = -1.57$ rad/s$^2$. Angular displacement: $\theta = 12.57 \times 8/2 = 50.27$ rad $\approx 8$ rev.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** converting between rpm and rad/s; applying the kinematic equations for constant angular acceleration; linking linear and angular quantities ($v = r\omega$, $a = r\omega^2$); finding number of revolutions from angular displacement.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Right-hand rule for angular quantities:** curl the fingers of your right hand in the direction of rotation; your thumb points along the angular velocity/acceleration vector. For anticlockwise rotation (as viewed from above), the vector points upward; for clockwise, it points downward.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Angular displacement is NOT a simple angle:** while measured in radians, angular displacement has direction (along the axis). For rotations greater than 2$\pi$, the magnitude keeps increasing — unlike a simple angle which resets after 360°. This is why angular displacement is a true vector quantity.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Non-uniform circular motion:** when angular acceleration $\alpha \neq 0$, both tangential acceleration ($a_t = r\alpha$) and centripetal acceleration ($a_c = r\omega^2$) are present. The total acceleration is the vector sum: $a = \sqrt{a_t^2 + a_c^2}$. The direction of total acceleration is at angle $\phi = \arctan(a_c/a_t)$ from the tangential direction.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Small angle approximation:** for oscillatory motion (pendulums, springs), when $\theta$ is small ($\theta < 0.1$ rad $\approx 6^\circ$), we can approximate $\sin \theta \approx \theta$ and $\cos \theta \approx 1$. This linearizes the equations and makes them solvable analytically.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Angular quantities as vectors — addition is NOT commutative for large angles:** while infinitesimal angular displacements $d\theta$ are vectors (they commute), finite angular displacements do NOT commute. Rotating 90° about x then 90° about y gives a different result than rotating 90° about y then 90° about x. This is why angular velocity is a true vector but finite angular displacement is not.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Angular displacement $\theta$:** the angle through which a point on a rotating body moves, measured in radians. $\theta = s/r$, where $s$ is arc length and $r$ is radius.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Angular velocity $\omega$:** rate of change of angular displacement. $\omega = d\theta/dt$. Units: rad/s.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Angular acceleration $\alpha$:** rate of change of angular velocity. $\alpha = d\omega/dt$. Units: rad/s$^2$.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>**Connection to linear motion:** $v = r\omega$ and $a_t = r\alpha$, where $v$ is tangential speed and $a_t$ is tangential acceleration.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>**Direction:** angular velocity and acceleration are vectors along the axis of rotation, given by the right-hand rule.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>**Uniform circular motion:** when $\omega$ is constant, angular displacement $\theta = \omega t$, angular acceleration $\alpha = 0$, but centripetal acceleration $a_c = r\omega^2$ is nonzero.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>**Constant angular acceleration:** rotational kinematic equations mirror translational ones: $\omega^2 = \omega_0^2 + 2\alpha\theta$, $\theta = \omega_0 t + \frac{1}{2}\alpha t^2$, $\omega = \omega_0 + \alpha t$.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>**Radian measure:** one complete revolution $= 2\pi$ radians $= 360^{\circ}$. All circular motion formulas require angles in radians, not degrees.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>**Frequency and period:** frequency $f = \omega/(2\pi)$ in Hz (revolutions per second). Period $T = 1/f = 2\pi/\omega$ is the time for one revolution.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>**Angular velocity in RPM:** to convert from revolutions per minute (RPM) to rad/s: $\omega = 2\pi N/60$, where $N$ is RPM.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>**Physical significance:** angular acceleration tells you how quickly the rotation speeds up or slows down. A spinning bicycle wheel with constant $\omega$ has $\alpha = 0$ but nonzero centripetal acceleration.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Angular displacement is measured in radians; 2π rad = 360° = 1 revolution.</td></tr><tr><td>Angular velocity is a vector along the axis (right-hand rule), not tangential.</td></tr><tr><td>The kinematic equations for rotation mirror linear motion: x→θ, v→ω, a→α.</td></tr><tr><td>v = rω links linear and angular speed — the basis of all circular motion problems.</td></tr><tr><td>Always convert rpm to rad/s before using formulas: multiply by 2π/60.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Angular displacement and angle are the same.' ✅ Angular displacement is a vector (along the axis), while angle is a scalar. For a full revolution, angular displacement = 2π rad, but the body returns to its starting position.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Angular velocity is always constant in circular motion.' ✅ Only in UNIFORM circular motion is ω constant. In non-uniform circular motion, ω changes and there is angular acceleration.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The direction of angular velocity is tangential.' ❌ The direction of angular velocity is ALONG THE AXIS of rotation (right-hand rule), not tangential. Tangential velocity is v = rω, but ω itself points along the axis.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Angular acceleration means the body is speeding up.' ✅ Angular acceleration is the rate of change of angular velocity. If α and ω have the same sign, the body speeds up; if opposite signs, it slows down.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The kinematic equations for rotation are different from linear motion.' ✅ They are IDENTICAL in form: just replace x with θ, v with ω, and a with α. The same problem-solving approach works.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'rpm and rad/s are the same.' ✅ 1 rpm = 2π/60 rad/s ≈ 0.105 rad/s. Always convert rpm to rad/s before using formulas.</td></tr><tr><td>Angular velocity $\omega$ is in rad/s, not m/s. The linear speed $v = r\omega$ converts angular velocity to linear speed at distance $r$ from the axis.</td></tr><tr><td>A body can have angular velocity without any linear velocity at all — every point on a body rotating about its own centre has zero linear velocity of the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>Angular acceleration $\alpha$ is zero in uniform circular motion, even though centripetal acceleration $a_c = r\omega^2$ is not. $\alpha$ changes the speed; $a_c$ changes the direction.</td></tr><tr><td>One full revolution $= 2\pi$ rad $= 360^{\circ}$. When using formulas like $\theta = \omega t$, angles must be in radians, not degrees.</td></tr><tr><td>The sign of $\omega$ or $\alpha$ indicates direction of rotation: positive for counterclockwise, negative for clockwise (by convention in the standard coordinate system).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Angular displacement (θ), angular velocity (ω = dθ/dt), and angular acceleration (α = dω/dt) describe rotational motion. For constant angular acceleration, the kinematic equations mirror linear motion. The links v = rω and a_c = rω² connect rotational and translational quantities. rpm is converted to rad/s by multiplying by 2π/60. Angular displacement, velocity and acceleration is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Centripetal Acceleration and Force</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Centripetal acceleration (a_c):** the acceleration directed toward the centre of a circular path that keeps a body moving in a circle. Magnitude: a_c = v²/r = rω². Direction: always toward the centre (hence 'centripetal' = 'centre-seeking'). It changes the direction of velocity, not its magnitude.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Centripetal force (F_c):** the net force required to produce centripetal acceleration: F_c = mv²/r = mrω². It is NOT a new force — it is the RESULTANT of real forces (tension, gravity, friction, normal) pointing toward the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Why centripetal force is not a separate force:** the centripetal force is always provided by one or more real forces. For a car turning on a flat road, friction provides F_c. For a planet orbiting the Sun, gravity provides F_c. For a ball on a string, tension provides F_c.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Derivation of a_c = v²/r:** consider a body moving at constant speed v in a circle of radius r. In a small time Δt, it moves from A to B. The change in velocity Δv points toward the centre. From similar triangles: Δv/v = Δs/r → Δv = vΔs/r. Acceleration a = Δv/Δt = v²/r (since Δs/Δt = v).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — car turning:** a 1000 kg car turns a flat curve of radius 50 m at 20 m/s. Required friction: F_c = mv²/r = 1000 × 400/50 = 8000 N. If μ_s = 0.6, max friction = 0.6 × 1000 × 9.8 = 5880 N — the car skids (8000 > 5880).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — ball on a string:** a 0.5 kg ball on a 1.5 m string rotates at 4 m/s. Tension = mv²/r = 0.5 × 16/1.5 = 5.33 N. If the string breaks at 10 N, max speed = √(10 × 1.5/0.5) = 5.48 m/s.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Worked example — planet orbit:** Earth orbits the Sun at 30 km/s at a distance of 1.5 × 10¹¹ m. Centripetal acceleration = v²/r = (3×10⁴)²/(1.5×10¹¹) = 6 × 10⁻³ m/s². This is provided by the Sun's gravity.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** calculating centripetal force/acceleration; identifying which real force provides F_c; maximum speed problems (string breaking, car skidding); linking v, ω, r, and a_c.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Centripetal force is not a force — it is a role:** the term 'centripetal force' describes the NET force pointing toward the center. It is always provided by some real force or combination of forces: tension (string), gravity (planets), friction (car on road), normal force (banked road), magnetic force (charged particle). Always draw an FBD and identify WHICH real force plays this role.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Centrifugal force in rotating frames:** in a reference frame attached to the rotating body (e.g., a car turning), passengers feel pushed outward. This is called centrifugal force. It is a pseudo-force — it arises from the inertia of the body wanting to move in a straight line. In an inertial (non-accelerating) frame, there is NO outward force — only the inward centripetal force.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Derivation via vector diagram:** consider a particle moving at constant speed v around a circle of radius r. After a small time Δt, it moves through angle Δθ = vΔt/r. The velocity vectors change direction by Δθ. From the similar triangles formed by position vectors and velocity vectors: |Δv|/v = |Δr|/r = Δs/r = vΔt/r. Therefore a = |Δv|/Δt = v²/r.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Worked example — satellite orbit:** a satellite orbits Earth at altitude 400 km (r = 6771 km) with speed 7.67 km/s. Centripetal acceleration: a_c = v²/r = (7670)²/6771000 = 8.69 m/s². This equals the local gravitational acceleration at that altitude, confirming that gravity provides the centripetal force.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Common NEB trap — direction of acceleration:** students often confuse the direction of centripetal acceleration (toward center) with the direction of velocity (tangential). Remember: a_c changes the DIRECTION of v, not its magnitude. If speed is changing too, there is also a tangential component a_t.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Centripetal force is NOT a new force — it is the resultant of real forces (tension, gravity, friction).</td></tr><tr><td>Centripetal acceleration changes only the direction of velocity, not its magnitude.</td></tr><tr><td>Centrifugal force is a pseudo-force that appears only in rotating reference frames.</td></tr><tr><td>If the centripetal force is removed, the body moves tangent to the circle.</td></tr><tr><td>Centripetal force is proportional to v² — doubling speed quadruples the required force.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Centrifugal force pulls the body outward.' ✅ Centrifugal force is a pseudo-force that appears only in a rotating reference frame. In an inertial frame, there is only centripetal force toward the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Centripetal force is a new type of force.' ✅ Centripetal force is the RESULTANT of real forces (tension, gravity, friction). It is not a separate force you add to the FBD.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The body in circular motion is in equilibrium.' ✅ The body accelerates toward the centre (a_c = v²/r). It is NOT in equilibrium — the net force is mv²/r toward the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Centripetal acceleration changes the speed.' ✅ Centripetal acceleration changes only the DIRECTION of velocity. Tangential acceleration (if present) changes the speed.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Centripetal force acts outward.' ✅ Centripetal force acts INWARD, toward the centre. The tendency to move outward is inertia, not a force.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'If the string breaks, the body moves radially outward.' ✅ If the string breaks, the body moves TANGENT to the circle (in the direction of its velocity at that instant), not radially.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Centripetal acceleration (a_c = v²/r = rω²) is directed toward the centre of a circular path. The centripetal force (F_c = mv²/r) is the resultant of real forces (tension, gravity, friction) — not a new force. Maximum-speed problems (string breaking, car skidding) and identifying which real force provides F_c are the key NEB topics.</p><hr><h2>Relation Between Angular and Linear Velocity and Acceleration</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Angular displacement (Δθ):** the angle through which a body rotates about a fixed axis. Measured in radians (rad). 1 rad = 57.3°, 2π rad = 360°. Angular displacement is a vector along the axis (right-hand rule): positive for anticlockwise rotation.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Angular velocity (ω):** the rate of change of angular displacement: ω = dθ/dt (instantaneous), ω = Δθ/t (average). Unit: rad/s. Direction: along the axis (right-hand rule). For uniform circular motion, ω is constant.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Angular acceleration (α):** the rate of change of angular velocity: α = dω/dt = d²θ/dt². Unit: rad/s². When α is constant, the kinematic equations mirror linear motion.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Relationship between linear and angular quantities (for circular motion):** v = rω (linear speed = radius × angular speed), a_t = rα (tangential acceleration), a_c = v²/r = rω² (centripetal acceleration). These links connect rotational and translational motion.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Kinematic equations for constant angular acceleration (mirror linear v = u + at, etc.):** ω = ω₀ + αt; θ = ω₀t + ½αt²; ω² = ω₀² + 2αθ; θ = (ω + ω₀)t/2. The structure is identical to linear motion — just swap x→θ, v→ω, a→α.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — spinning wheel:** a wheel starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 2 rad/s² for 5 s. Angular displacement: θ = 0 + ½ × 2 × 25 = 25 rad. Final angular velocity: ω = 0 + 2 × 5 = 10 rad/s. Number of revolutions: 25/(2π) ≈ 3.98 rev.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Worked example — decelerating fan:** a fan blade rotating at 120 rpm (120 × 2π/60 = 12.57 rad/s) comes to rest in 8 s. Angular acceleration: α = (0 − 12.57)/8 = −1.57 rad/s². Angular displacement: θ = 12.57 × 8/2 = 50.27 rad ≈ 8 rev.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** converting between rpm and rad/s; applying the kinematic equations for constant angular acceleration; linking linear and angular quantities (v = rω, a = rω²); finding number of revolutions from angular displacement.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Right-hand rule for angular quantities:** curl the fingers of your right hand in the direction of rotation; your thumb points along the angular velocity/acceleration vector. For anticlockwise rotation (as viewed from above), the vector points upward; for clockwise, it points downward.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Angular displacement is NOT a simple angle:** while measured in radians, angular displacement has direction (along the axis). For rotations greater than 2π, the magnitude keeps increasing — unlike a simple angle which resets after 360°. This is why angular displacement is a true vector quantity.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Non-uniform circular motion:** when angular acceleration α ≠ 0, both tangential acceleration (a_t = rα) and centripetal acceleration (a_c = rω²) are present. The total acceleration is the vector sum: a = √(a_t² + a_c²). The direction of total acceleration is at angle φ = arctan(a_c/a_t) from the tangential direction.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Small angle approximation:** for oscillatory motion (pendulums, springs), when θ is small (θ < 0.1 rad ≈ 6°), we can approximate sin θ ≈ θ and cos θ ≈ 1. This linearizes the equations and makes them solvable analytically.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Angular quantities as vectors — addition is NOT commutative for large angles:** while infinitesimal angular displacements dθ are vectors (they commute), finite angular displacements do NOT commute. Rotating 90° about x then 90° about y gives a different result than rotating 90° about y then 90° about x. This is why angular velocity is a true vector but finite angular displacement is not.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Linear to angular:** $\omega = v/r$. A point at the rim of a wheel moves faster in linear speed but has the same angular velocity as a point near the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Angular to linear:** $v = r\omega$. If $\omega$ is constant, linear speed increases with radius — which is why the outer edge of a spinning disc has higher speed.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Tangential acceleration:** $a_t = r\alpha$. This is the acceleration tangent to the circle, changing the speed along the path.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>**Centripetal acceleration:** $a_c = v^2/r = r\omega^2$. This is the inward acceleration required to keep the object moving in a circle.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>**Total acceleration:** when both speed and direction change, $\vec{a} = \vec{a}_t + \vec{a}_c$. The two components are perpendicular.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>**Direction of $\vec{v}$:** the linear velocity is always tangential to the circular path. The direction changes continuously even when speed is constant.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>**Instantaneous vs average:** $v = r\omega$ relates instantaneous values. For average values, $\bar{v} = r\bar{\omega}$ holds only if angular acceleration is constant.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>**Vector form:** $\vec{v} = \vec{\omega} \times \vec{r}$. This cross product automatically gives the correct direction (tangential) and magnitude ($r\omega$).</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>**Multiple radii on one body:** all points on a rigid rotating body share the same $\omega$ and $\alpha$, but their tangential speed and tangential acceleration differ proportionally to their distance from the axis.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>**Practical example:** a grinding wheel at 3000 RPM with diameter 0.2 m: $\omega = 3000 \times 2\pi/60 = 100\pi \approx 314$ rad/s. Rim speed $v = 0.1 \times 314 = 31.4$ m/s, fast enough to be dangerous.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Angular displacement is measured in radians; 2π rad = 360° = 1 revolution.</td></tr><tr><td>Angular velocity is a vector along the axis (right-hand rule), not tangential.</td></tr><tr><td>The kinematic equations for rotation mirror linear motion: x→θ, v→ω, a→α.</td></tr><tr><td>v = rω links linear and angular speed — the basis of all circular motion problems.</td></tr><tr><td>Always convert rpm to rad/s before using formulas: multiply by 2π/60.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Angular displacement and angle are the same.' ✅ Angular displacement is a vector (along the axis), while angle is a scalar. For a full revolution, angular displacement = 2π rad, but the body returns to its starting position.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Angular velocity is always constant in circular motion.' ✅ Only in UNIFORM circular motion is ω constant. In non-uniform circular motion, ω changes and there is angular acceleration.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The direction of angular velocity is tangential.' ❌ The direction of angular velocity is ALONG THE AXIS of rotation (right-hand rule), not tangential. Tangential velocity is v = rω, but ω itself points along the axis.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Angular acceleration means the body is speeding up.' ✅ Angular acceleration is the rate of change of angular velocity. If α and ω have the same sign, the body speeds up; if opposite signs, it slows down.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The kinematic equations for rotation are different from linear motion.' ✅ They are IDENTICAL in form: just replace x with θ, v with ω, and a with α. The same problem-solving approach works.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'rpm and rad/s are the same.' ✅ 1 rpm = 2π/60 rad/s ≈ 0.105 rad/s. Always convert rpm to rad/s before using formulas.</td></tr><tr><td>All points on a rigid body have the same angular velocity $\omega$, but points farther from the axis have greater linear speed $v = r\omega$. The relation $v = r\omega$ does not mean larger radius means larger $\omega$.</td></tr><tr><td>Centripetal acceleration $a_c = v^2/r$ depends on $v$ at that radius. Since $v = r\omega$, substituting gives $a_c = r\omega^2$, which increases with $r$. Do not confuse with $v^2/r$ appearing to decrease with $r$ — that is for fixed $v$, not fixed $\omega$.</td></tr><tr><td>Tangential acceleration $a_t = r\alpha$ changes the speed. Centripetal acceleration $a_c = r\omega^2$ changes the direction. They are perpendicular and independent — you can have one without the other.</td></tr><tr><td>The formula $\vec{v} = \vec{\omega} \times \vec{r}$ is the vector relationship. In one dimension, it simplifies to $v = r\omega$. But the vector form is essential for 3D problems.</td></tr><tr><td>For a rolling wheel, the velocity of the top is $v + r\omega$ (which equals $2v$) and the bottom is $v - r\omega = 0$ (instantaneous rest for rolling without slipping).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Angular displacement (θ), angular velocity (ω = dθ/dt), and angular acceleration (α = dω/dt) describe rotational motion. For constant angular acceleration, the kinematic equations mirror linear motion. The links v = rω and a_c = rω² connect rotational and translational quantities. rpm is converted to rad/s by multiplying by 2π/60. Relation between angular and linear velocity and acceleration is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Centripetal Acceleration and Centripetal Force</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Centripetal acceleration (a_c):** the acceleration directed toward the centre of a circular path that keeps a body moving in a circle. Magnitude: a_c = v²/r = rω². Direction: always toward the centre (hence 'centripetal' = 'centre-seeking'). It changes the direction of velocity, not its magnitude.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Centripetal force (F_c):** the net force required to produce centripetal acceleration: F_c = mv²/r = mrω². It is NOT a new force — it is the RESULTANT of real forces (tension, gravity, friction, normal) pointing toward the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Why centripetal force is not a separate force:** the centripetal force is always provided by one or more real forces. For a car turning on a flat road, friction provides F_c. For a planet orbiting the Sun, gravity provides F_c. For a ball on a string, tension provides F_c.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Derivation of a_c = v²/r:** consider a body moving at constant speed v in a circle of radius r. In a small time Δt, it moves from A to B. The change in velocity Δv points toward the centre. From similar triangles: Δv/v = Δs/r → Δv = vΔs/r. Acceleration a = Δv/Δt = v²/r (since Δs/Δt = v).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — car turning:** a 1000 kg car turns a flat curve of radius 50 m at 20 m/s. Required friction: F_c = mv²/r = 1000 × 400/50 = 8000 N. If μ_s = 0.6, max friction = 0.6 × 1000 × 9.8 = 5880 N — the car skids (8000 > 5880).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — ball on a string:** a 0.5 kg ball on a 1.5 m string rotates at 4 m/s. Tension = mv²/r = 0.5 × 16/1.5 = 5.33 N. If the string breaks at 10 N, max speed = √(10 × 1.5/0.5) = 5.48 m/s.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Worked example — planet orbit:** Earth orbits the Sun at 30 km/s at a distance of 1.5 × 10¹¹ m. Centripetal acceleration = v²/r = (3×10⁴)²/(1.5×10¹¹) = 6 × 10⁻³ m/s². This is provided by the Sun's gravity.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** calculating centripetal force/acceleration; identifying which real force provides F_c; maximum speed problems (string breaking, car skidding); linking v, ω, r, and a_c.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Centripetal force is not a force — it is a role:** the term 'centripetal force' describes the NET force pointing toward the center. It is always provided by some real force or combination of forces: tension (string), gravity (planets), friction (car on road), normal force (banked road), magnetic force (charged particle). Always draw an FBD and identify WHICH real force plays this role.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Centrifugal force in rotating frames:** in a reference frame attached to the rotating body (e.g., a car turning), passengers feel pushed outward. This is called centrifugal force. It is a pseudo-force — it arises from the inertia of the body wanting to move in a straight line. In an inertial (non-accelerating) frame, there is NO outward force — only the inward centripetal force.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Derivation via vector diagram:** consider a particle moving at constant speed v around a circle of radius r. After a small time Δt, it moves through angle Δθ = vΔt/r. The velocity vectors change direction by Δθ. From the similar triangles formed by position vectors and velocity vectors: |Δv|/v = |Δr|/r = Δs/r = vΔt/r. Therefore a = |Δv|/Δt = v²/r.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Worked example — satellite orbit:** a satellite orbits Earth at altitude 400 km (r = 6771 km) with speed 7.67 km/s. Centripetal acceleration: a_c = v²/r = (7670)²/6771000 = 8.69 m/s². This equals the local gravitational acceleration at that altitude, confirming that gravity provides the centripetal force.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Common NEB trap — direction of acceleration:** students often confuse the direction of centripetal acceleration (toward center) with the direction of velocity (tangential). Remember: a_c changes the DIRECTION of v, not its magnitude. If speed is changing too, there is also a tangential component a_t.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Centripetal force is NOT a new force — it is the resultant of real forces (tension, gravity, friction).</td></tr><tr><td>Centripetal acceleration changes only the direction of velocity, not its magnitude.</td></tr><tr><td>Centrifugal force is a pseudo-force that appears only in rotating reference frames.</td></tr><tr><td>If the centripetal force is removed, the body moves tangent to the circle.</td></tr><tr><td>Centripetal force is proportional to v² — doubling speed quadruples the required force.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Centrifugal force pulls the body outward.' ✅ Centrifugal force is a pseudo-force that appears only in a rotating reference frame. In an inertial frame, there is only centripetal force toward the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Centripetal force is a new type of force.' ✅ Centripetal force is the RESULTANT of real forces (tension, gravity, friction). It is not a separate force you add to the FBD.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The body in circular motion is in equilibrium.' ✅ The body accelerates toward the centre (a_c = v²/r). It is NOT in equilibrium — the net force is mv²/r toward the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Centripetal acceleration changes the speed.' ✅ Centripetal acceleration changes only the DIRECTION of velocity. Tangential acceleration (if present) changes the speed.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Centripetal force acts outward.' ✅ Centripetal force acts INWARD, toward the centre. The tendency to move outward is inertia, not a force.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'If the string breaks, the body moves radially outward.' ✅ If the string breaks, the body moves TANGENT to the circle (in the direction of its velocity at that instant), not radially.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Centripetal acceleration (a_c = v²/r = rω²) is directed toward the centre of a circular path. The centripetal force (F_c = mv²/r) is the resultant of real forces (tension, gravity, friction) — not a new force. Maximum-speed problems (string breaking, car skidding) and identifying which real force provides F_c are the key NEB topics.</p><hr><h2>Conical Pendulum</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Conical pendulum:** a small bob of mass m attached to a string of length L, moving in a horizontal circle at constant speed such that the string traces out a cone. The string makes angle θ with the vertical and the bob moves in a circle of radius r = L sin θ.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Forces and geometry:** two forces act on the bob: tension T along the string, and weight mg downward. The tension has vertical component T cos θ (balances weight) and horizontal component T sin θ (provides centripetal force).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Key equations:** T cos θ = mg (vertical equilibrium) ... (1); T sin θ = mω²r = mω²L sin θ (centripetal) ... (2). From (2): T = mω²L. Substituting into (1): mω²L cos θ = mg → cos θ = g/(ω²L). Also: ω = √(g/L cos θ) and T_period = 2π√(L cos θ/g).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Key insight — period depends only on L cos θ:** the period T = 2π√(L cos θ/g) is independent of the mass of the bob. A taller cone (larger θ, smaller cos θ) has a shorter period (faster rotation). The minimum period is when θ → 0: T_min = 2π√(L/g) (same as a simple pendulum of length L).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example:** a 0.2 kg bob on a 2 m string rotates with θ = 30°. cos 30° = 0.866. Angular speed: ω = √(9.8/(2 × 0.866)) = √5.66 = 2.38 rad/s. Period: T = 2π/2.38 = 2.64 s. Tension: T = mω²L = 0.2 × 5.66 × 2 = 2.26 N. Radius: r = 2 × sin 30° = 1 m.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Comparison with simple pendulum:** both have period T = 2π√(L_eff/g). For a simple pendulum, L_eff = L. For a conical pendulum, L_eff = L cos θ (the vertical height of the cone). So a conical pendulum swings slower than a simple pendulum of the same string length.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** deriving the relation cos θ = g/(ω²L); finding the period, angular speed, or tension; showing that the period is independent of mass; comparing conical and simple pendulums.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Practical examples:** a tethered aeroplane model, a ball on a string swung in a horizontal circle, vehicles banking on a curved road (same geometry — the road provides the 'tension' component).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Deriving the key result step by step:** from vertical equilibrium: T cos θ = mg → T = mg/cos θ. From horizontal force: T sin θ = mω²r = mω²L sin θ. Canceling sin θ (assuming θ ≠ 0): T = mω²L. Equating: mω²L = mg/cos θ → ω² = g/(L cos θ) → ω = √(g/L cos θ). This derivation is the standard NEB proof question.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Period formula insight:** T_period = 2π/ω = 2π√(L cos θ/g). Note that L cos θ is the VERTICAL HEIGHT h of the cone. So T = 2π√(h/g) — the period depends only on the vertical height, not on the radius or the mass. A cone with height 1 m has the same period regardless of how wide it is.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Relation to simple pendulum:** a simple pendulum of length L has period T = 2π√(L/g). A conical pendulum of string length L has period T = 2π√(L cos θ/g) = 2π√(h/g). Since cos θ < 1, the conical pendulum's period is LONGER than a simple pendulum of the same string length — it swings more slowly.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Connection to banking of roads:** the conical pendulum and a banked road share identical force geometry. In the pendulum, tension provides both vertical support and centripetal force. In a banked road, the normal force plays the same dual role. The equation tan θ = v²/(rg) for banking is equivalent to the conical pendulum's geometry.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Watt's governor:** the conical pendulum is the basis of the centrifugal governor used in steam engines. As the engine speeds up, the bobs rise (θ increases), which mechanically reduces the steam supply. This is a real-world application that NEB sometimes references.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Conical pendulum:** a small bob of mass $m$ attached to a string of length $L$, moving in a horizontal circle at constant speed such that the string traces out a cone. The string makes angle $\theta$ with the vertical and the bob moves in a circle of radius $r = L \sin\theta$.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Forces and geometry:** two forces act on the bob: tension $T$ along the string, and weight $mg$ downward. The tension has vertical component $T \cos\theta$ (balances weight) and horizontal component $T \sin\theta$ (provides centripetal force).</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Key equations:** $T \cos\theta = mg$ (vertical equilibrium) ... (1); $T \sin\theta = m\omega^2 r = m\omega^2 L \sin\theta$ (centripetal) ... (2). From (2): $T = m\omega^2 L$. Substituting into (1): $m\omega^2 L \cos\theta = mg$ → $T \cos\theta = mg$. Also: $\omega = \sqrt{\frac{g}{L \cos\theta}}$ and $T_{period} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L \cos\theta}{g}}$.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>**Key insight — period depends only on $L \cos\theta$:** the period $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L \cos\theta}{g}}$ is independent of the mass of the bob. A taller cone (larger $\theta$, smaller $\cos\theta$) has a shorter period (faster rotation). The minimum period is when $\theta \to 0$: $T_{min} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}$ (same as a simple pendulum of length $L$).</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>**Worked example:** a $0.2\text{ kg}$ bob on a $2\text{ m}$ string rotates with $\theta = 30^\circ$. $\cos 30^\circ = 0.866$. Angular speed: $\omega = \sqrt{\frac{9.8}{2 \times 0.866}} = \sqrt{5.66} = 2.38\text{ rad/s}$. Period: $T = 2\pi/2.38 = 2.64\text{ s}$. Tension: $T = m\omega^2 L = 0.2 \times 5.66 \times 2 = 2.26\text{ N}$. Radius: $r = 2 \times \sin 30^\circ = 1\text{ m}$.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>**Comparison with simple pendulum:** both have period $T = 2\pi\sqrt{L_{\mathrm{eff}}/g}$. For a simple pendulum, $L_{\mathrm{eff}} = L$. For a conical pendulum, $L_{\mathrm{eff}} = L \cos\theta$ (the vertical height of the cone). So a conical pendulum swings slower than a simple pendulum of the same string length.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** deriving the relation $\cos\theta = \frac{g}{\omega^2 L}$; finding the period, angular speed, or tension; showing that the period is independent of mass; comparing conical and simple pendulums.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>**Deriving the key result step by step:** from vertical equilibrium: $T \cos\theta = mg \implies T = \frac{mg}{\cos\theta}$. From horizontal force: $T \sin\theta = m\omega^2 r = m\omega^2 L \sin\theta$. Canceling $\sin\theta$ (assuming $\theta \neq 0$): $T = m\omega^2 L$. Equating: $m\omega^2 L = \frac{mg}{\cos\theta} \implies \omega^2 = \frac{g}{L \cos\theta} \implies \omega = \sqrt{\frac{g}{L \cos\theta}}$. This derivation is the standard NEB proof question.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>**Period formula insight:** $T_{period} = \frac{2\pi}{\omega} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L \cos\theta}{g}}$. Note that $L \cos\theta$ is the VERTICAL HEIGHT $h$ of the cone. So $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{h}{g}}$ — the period depends only on the vertical height, not on the radius or the mass. A cone with height $1\text{ m}$ has the same period regardless of how wide it is.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>**Relation to simple pendulum:** a simple pendulum of length $L$ has period $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}$. A conical pendulum of string length $L$ has period $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L \cos\theta}{g}} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{h}{g}}$. Since $\cos\theta < 1$, the conical pendulum's period is LONGER than a simple pendulum of the same string length — it swings more slowly.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>**Connection to banking of roads:** the conical pendulum and a banked road share identical force geometry. In the pendulum, tension provides both vertical support and centripetal force. In a banked road, the normal force plays the same dual role. The equation $\tan\theta = \frac{v^2}{rg}$ for banking is equivalent to the conical pendulum's geometry.</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>**Setup:** a mass $m$ attached to a string of length $l$ rotates in a horizontal circle, making a constant angle $\theta$ with the vertical.</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>**Forces:** tension $T$ has components $T\cos\theta$ upward balancing weight $mg$, and $T\sin\theta$ inward providing centripetal force.</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>**Period:** $T_{period} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{l\cos\theta}{g}}$. The period depends on the string length, the angle, and gravity.</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>**Special case — small angles:** when $\theta$ is small, $\cos\theta \approx 1$, so $T_{period} \approx 2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$, the same as a simple pendulum.</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>**Speed:** $v = \sqrt{gr\tan\theta}$, where $r = l\sin\theta$ is the radius of the horizontal circle. Faster spin means a larger angle $\theta$.</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>**Height of the pendulum:** the vertical distance from the suspension point to the mass is $h = l\cos\theta$. The period depends only on this height: $T_{period} = 2\pi\sqrt{h/g}$.</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>**Angular velocity:** $\omega = \sqrt{g/(l\cos\theta)} = \sqrt{g/h}$. As the mass spins faster, $\theta$ increases, $h$ decreases, and the period decreases.</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>**Tension:** from $T\cos\theta = mg$, we get $T = mg/\cos\theta$. As $\theta$ increases, tension increases without bound. At $\theta = 90^{\circ}$, tension would be infinite — physically impossible.</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>**Maximum angle limit:** as $\omega \to \infty$, $\theta \to 90^{\circ}$ but never reaches it. The mass rises toward the horizontal but cannot get there because the required tension would be infinite.</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>**Analogy with a spinning conical top:** the path swept by the string traces a cone, hence the name. The path on a horizontal plane is circular, but the full trajectory in 3D is the surface of a cone.</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>**Relation to simple pendulum:** a conical pendulum with very small $\theta$ behaves like a simple pendulum of the same length, oscillating back and forth rather than spinning around.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>The period of a conical pendulum depends only on L cos θ (the vertical height of the cone), not on mass.</td></tr><tr><td>The faster the rotation, the larger the angle θ — the cone becomes flatter.</td></tr><tr><td>The minimum period (θ → 0) equals the period of a simple pendulum of the same length.</td></tr><tr><td>The tension is always greater than mg: T = mg/cos θ.</td></tr><tr><td>The centripetal force is the horizontal component of tension — no new force is added.</td></tr><tr><td>The period of a conical pendulum depends only on $L \cos\theta$ (the vertical height of the cone), not on mass.</td></tr><tr><td>The faster the rotation, the larger the angle $\theta$ — the cone becomes flatter.</td></tr><tr><td>The minimum period ($\theta \to 0$) equals the period of a simple pendulum of the same length.</td></tr><tr><td>The tension is always greater than $mg$: $T = \frac{mg}{\cos\theta}$.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The tension in the string equals mg.' ✅ The vertical component of tension equals mg. The total tension is greater: T = mg/cos θ (since cos θ < 1, T > mg).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The bob is in equilibrium.' ✅ The bob is in vertical equilibrium (T cos θ = mg) but NOT horizontal equilibrium — it accelerates toward the centre (centripetal acceleration).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The period depends on the mass of the bob.' ✅ The period T = 2π√(L cos θ/g) is independent of mass. A heavier bob rotates at the same period (for the same θ).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A faster rotation gives a smaller θ.' ✅ A FASTER rotation gives a LARGER θ (the cone becomes flatter). From cos θ = g/(ω²L), as ω increases, cos θ decreases, so θ increases.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The radius of the circle is L.' ✅ The radius is r = L sin θ, which is LESS than L. Only if θ = 90° would r = L (but then cos θ = 0 and the equations break down).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The centripetal force is a new force to add to the FBD.' ✅ The centripetal force IS the horizontal component of tension: T sin θ = mω²r. No new force is added.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The tension in the string equals mg.' ✅ The vertical component of tension equals mg. The total tension is greater: $T = \frac{mg}{\cos\theta}$ (since $\cos\theta < 1$, $T > mg$).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The bob is in equilibrium.' ✅ The bob is in vertical equilibrium ($T \cos\theta = mg$) but NOT horizontal equilibrium — it accelerates toward the centre (centripetal acceleration).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The period depends on the mass of the bob.' ✅ The period $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L \cos\theta}{g}}$ is independent of mass. A heavier bob rotates at the same period (for the same $\theta$).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A faster rotation gives a smaller θ.' ✅ A FASTER rotation gives a LARGER θ (the cone becomes flatter). From $\cos\theta = \frac{g}{\omega^2 L}$, as $\omega$ increases, $\cos\theta$ decreases, so $\theta$ increases.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The radius of the circle is L.' ✅ The radius is $r = L \sin\theta$, which is LESS than $L$. Only if $\theta = 90^\circ$ would $r = L$ (but then $\cos\theta = 0$ and the equations break down).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The centripetal force is a new force to add to the FBD.' ✅ The centripetal force IS the horizontal component of tension: $T \sin\theta = m\omega^2 r$. No new force is added.</td></tr><tr><td>The period of a conical pendulum $T_{period} = 2\pi\sqrt{l\cos\theta/g}$ is always less than that of a simple pendulum of the same length ($2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$) because $\cos\theta < 1$.</td></tr><tr><td>Tension in a conical pendulum is always greater than $mg$ because $T = mg/\cos\theta$ and $\cos\theta < 1$. The faster the rotation, the greater the tension.</td></tr><tr><td>The period of a conical pendulum decreases as it spins faster (larger $\theta$), unlike a simple pendulum where period depends only on length and $g$.</td></tr><tr><td>The period depends on the vertical height $h = l\cos\theta$, not on the radius of the circle. Two conical pendulums with the same $h$ have the same period regardless of their lengths.</td></tr><tr><td>The conical pendulum is not a 2D problem. The mass moves in a horizontal circle, but the string sweeps out a cone in 3D space.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A conical pendulum consists of a bob moving in a horizontal circle with the string tracing a cone. The key equations are T cos θ = mg and T sin θ = mω²L sin θ, giving ω = √(g/L cos θ) and T_period = 2π√(L cos θ/g). The period is independent of the bob's mass and depends only on the vertical height of the cone. A conical pendulum consists of a bob moving in a horizontal circle with the string tracing a cone. The key equations are $T \cos\theta = mg$ and $T \sin\theta = m\omega^2 L \sin\theta$, giving $\omega = \sqrt{\frac{g}{L \cos\theta}}$ and $T_{period} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L \cos\theta}{g}}$. The period is independent of the bob's mass and depends only on the vertical height of the cone. Conical pendulum is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Motion in a Vertical Circle</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Motion in a vertical circle:** a body of mass m attached to a string moves in a vertical plane under gravity. Unlike horizontal circular motion, the speed varies with height — fastest at the bottom, slowest at the top.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Why speed varies:** gravity does work on the body as it moves vertically. Kinetic energy is converted to gravitational PE on the way up, and back to KE on the way down. Total mechanical energy is conserved (ignoring air resistance).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Critical distinction — string vs rigid rod:** a string can only pull (tension ≥ 0), so the body must have enough speed at the top to keep the string taut. A rigid rod can push or pull, so the body can reach the top with zero speed.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Minimum speed at the top (string):** at the top, both tension T and weight mg point downward: T + mg = mv²/r. The minimum speed occurs when T → 0: mg = mv_min²/r → v_top(min) = √(gr). Below this, the string goes slack.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Minimum speed at the bottom (string):** using energy conservation from bottom to top: ½mv_bottom² = ½mv_top² + mg(2r). With v_top = √(gr): ½v_bottom² = ½(gr) + 2gr → v_bottom² = 5gr → v_bottom(min) = √(5gr). This is the critical NEB result.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Minimum speed at the bottom (rod):** since the rod can push, the body can reach the top with v_top = 0. Energy conservation: ½v_bottom² = 0 + 2gr → v_bottom(min) = √(2gr). This is lower than the string case.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Tension at the bottom:** T_bottom − mg = mv_bottom²/r → T_bottom = mg + mv_bottom²/r. For the minimum case (string): T_bottom = mg + 5mg = 6mg. The tension at the bottom is 6 times the weight.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Tension at the top:** T_top + mg = mv_top²/r → T_top = mv_top²/r − mg. For the minimum case (string): T_top = 0.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Worked example:** a 0.5 kg bob on a 1.5 m string. v_top(min) = √(9.8 × 1.5) = 3.83 m/s. v_bottom(min) = √(5 × 9.8 × 1.5) = 8.57 m/s. T_bottom = 6 × 0.5 × 9.8 = 29.4 N.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** deriving v_top(min) = √(gr) and v_bottom(min) = √(5gr); comparing string vs rod; finding tension at top and bottom; energy conservation between positions.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Energy diagram for vertical circle:** plot KE, PE, and total E vs. height. KE is maximum at the bottom (½mv_b²), minimum at the top (½mv_t²). PE is minimum (0) at the bottom and maximum (mg·2r) at the top. Total energy E = ½mv_b² = ½mv_t² + 2mgr — a horizontal line on the diagram.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Tension variation around the circle:** at any angle θ from the bottom, T = mv²/r + mg cos θ. At the bottom (θ = 0°): T = mg + mv²/r (maximum). At the top (θ = 180°): T = mv²/r − mg (minimum). The tension difference between bottom and top is always 6mg when the minimum-speed condition holds.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Worked example — tension at arbitrary point:** a 0.2 kg bob on a 0.8 m string has speed 6 m/s at the bottom. At the top: ½v_t² = ½ × 36 − 9.8 × 1.6 = 18 − 15.68 = 2.32 → v_t = 2.15 m/s. T_top = 0.2 × 4.62/0.8 − 1.96 = 1.16 − 1.96 = −0.80 N. Since T < 0, the string goes slack before reaching the top. The bob cannot complete the circle.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Rod vs. string — key differences:** a rod can support compression, so the bob can reach the top with v = 0. The rod pushes upward with force mg at the top. Minimum bottom speed for a rod: √(2gr). For a string: √(5gr). The factor 5 versus 2 is the critical distinction tested in NEB.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Complete-the-circle condition:** for a string, the condition is v_bottom ≥ √(5gr). For a rod, it is v_bottom ≥ √(2gr). If v_bottom is between √(2gr) and √(5gr), the bob completes the circle with a rod but not with a string.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>The minimum speed at the top (string) is √(gr), independent of mass.</td></tr><tr><td>The minimum speed at the bottom (string) is √(5gr) — the most tested result.</td></tr><tr><td>For a rigid rod, v_bottom(min) = √(2gr) — the rod can push, not just pull.</td></tr><tr><td>Tension at the bottom is always maximum; at the top it is minimum.</td></tr><tr><td>At the minimum case (string), T_bottom = 6mg — six times the weight.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The speed is constant in vertical circular motion.' ✅ The speed VARIES — gravity speeds up the body on the way down and slows it on the way up. Only energy is conserved.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The minimum speed at the top is zero.' ✅ For a STRING, the minimum speed at the top is √(gr). For a ROD, it is indeed zero.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The tension is the same at all points.' ✅ The tension varies — maximum at the bottom (T = mg + mv²/r) and minimum at the top (T = mv²/r − mg).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The minimum speed at the bottom is √(2gr).' ✅ That is for a ROD. For a STRING, it is √(5gr) — the body must have enough energy to reach the top with speed √(gr).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'If the string goes slack, the body stops.' ✅ If the string goes slack, the body becomes a projectile — it follows a parabolic path under gravity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The centripetal force is constant.' ✅ The centripetal force (mv²/r) varies because v varies. At the bottom, it is maximum; at the top, it is minimum.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Motion in a vertical circle involves varying speed due to gravity. For a string, the minimum speed at the top is √(gr) and at the bottom is √(5gr). A rigid rod allows v_top = 0, giving v_bottom(min) = √(2gr). Tension is maximum at the bottom (T = mg + mv²/r) and minimum at the top. Energy conservation links speeds at different heights.</p><hr><h2>Applications of Banking</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Banking of roads:** the practice of raising the outer edge of a curved road above the inner edge so that the normal reaction from the road surface provides part or all of the centripetal force needed to turn. This reduces reliance on friction and prevents skidding.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Why banking is needed:** on a flat road, the entire centripetal force must come from friction: F_c = μmg. At high speeds or on slippery roads, friction may be insufficient and the vehicle skids outward. Banking tilts the road so the normal force has a horizontal component pointing toward the center.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Forces on a banked road:** the road is tilted at angle θ. The normal reaction N acts perpendicular to the road surface. Resolving N: vertical component N cos θ balances weight (N cos θ = mg); horizontal component N sin θ provides centripetal force (N sin θ = mv²/r).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Key equation — ideal banking angle (no friction needed):** dividing the two equations: tan θ = v²/(rg). For a given radius r and design speed v, the road should be banked at angle θ = arctan(v²/(rg)). At this angle, no friction is required.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Friction on a banked road:** if the vehicle moves faster than the design speed, friction acts DOWN the slope (preventing outward skid). If slower, friction acts UP the slope (preventing inward sliding). The general formula: tan(θ + φ) = v²/(rg), where φ = arctan(μ).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Maximum speed on a banked road with friction:** v_max = √[rg(tan θ + μ)/(1 − μ tan θ)]. When μ = 0 (no friction), this reduces to v = √(rg tan θ). When θ = 0 (flat road), it reduces to v = √(μrg).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Worked example — car on banked curve:** a road of radius 100 m is banked at θ = 15°. Design speed (no friction): v = √(100 × 9.8 × tan 15°) = √(100 × 9.8 × 0.268) = √262.6 = 16.2 m/s (≈ 58 km/h).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Worked example — with friction:** same road with μ = 0.3. v_max = √[100 × 9.8 × (0.268 + 0.3)/(1 − 0.3 × 0.268)] = √[980 × 0.568 / 0.92] = √605 = 24.6 m/s (≈ 89 km/h).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Railway banking (superelevation):** railways use the same principle. The outer rail is raised by height h above the inner rail. For track gauge w and banking angle θ: tan θ ≈ h/w. This prevents wear on rails and ensures passenger comfort.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** deriving tan θ = v²/(rg); finding the design speed for a given banking angle; maximum speed with friction; railway superelevation problems.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Force resolution on a banked road — detailed derivation:** draw the FBD with N perpendicular to the road and mg vertically down. Resolve N into: vertical component N cos θ (balances weight) and horizontal component N sin θ (provides centripetal force). From N cos θ = mg: N = mg/cos θ. From N sin θ = mv²/r: (mg/cos θ) sin θ = mv²/r → mg tan θ = mv²/r → tan θ = v²/(rg). This derivation is the standard NEB proof.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Friction on a banked road — general case:** when friction is present, there are two limiting cases. Case 1 (going too fast): friction acts DOWN the slope, adding to the centripetal force. Case 2 (going too slow): friction acts UP the slope, preventing inward sliding. The general equation combining both: N sin θ ± f cos θ = mv²/r (horizontal) and N cos θ ∓ f sin θ = mg (vertical), where f = μN.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Worked example — finding banking angle:** a curve of radius 150 m is designed for 90 km/h (25 m/s). Required banking angle: tan θ = v²/(rg) = 625/(150 × 9.8) = 0.425 → θ = 23.0°. At this angle, a car can negotiate the curve without any friction.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Worked example — height of outer rail:** a railway track of gauge 1.5 m is banked at 5°. The height by which the outer rail is raised: h = w tan θ = 1.5 × tan 5° = 1.5 × 0.0875 = 0.131 m ≈ 13.1 cm. This is a typical NEB numerical problem.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Why banking reduces tire wear:** on a flat road, friction must provide all centripetal force, causing lateral tire stress. On a banked road at the design speed, the normal force provides the centripetal component — no lateral friction needed. This dramatically reduces tire wear and fuel consumption.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>The ideal banking angle tan θ = v²/(rg) is independent of vehicle mass.</td></tr><tr><td>At design speed, no friction is needed — the horizontal component of normal force provides all centripetal force.</td></tr><tr><td>Banking with friction increases the maximum safe speed beyond the design speed.</td></tr><tr><td>Railway superelevation h = w tan θ applies the same physics to train tracks.</td></tr><tr><td>Over-banking (θ too large for the speed) causes inward sliding; under-banking causes outward skidding.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Banking eliminates friction entirely.' ✅ Banking REDUCES friction requirement. At the design speed (tan θ = v²/(rg)), friction is zero. At other speeds, friction is still needed.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A steeper bank angle is always better.' ✅ Too steep a bank angle causes problems at low speeds — the vehicle may slide inward. The ideal angle depends on the design speed.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The normal force equals mg on a banked road.' ✅ The normal force is N = mg/cos θ, which is GREATER than mg because the road is tilted.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Banking angle depends on the mass of the vehicle.' ✅ The ideal banking angle tan θ = v²/(rg) is independent of mass — it depends only on speed and radius.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'On a banked road, friction always acts up the slope.' ✅ Friction direction depends on speed: if v > design speed, friction acts DOWN the slope; if v < design speed, friction acts UP.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A higher banking angle means a lower maximum speed.' ✅ A HIGHER banking angle INCREASES the maximum speed — more of the normal force contributes to centripetal force.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Banking of roads tilts the road surface so the normal force provides part or all of the centripetal force. The ideal angle (no friction needed) is tan θ = v²/(rg), independent of mass. With friction, the maximum speed is v_max = √[rg(tan θ + μ)/(1 − μ tan θ)]. Railway tracks use superelevation h = w tan θ. Banking prevents skidding and reduces tire wear.</p><hr><h2>Motion in a Vertical Circle</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Motion in a vertical circle:** a body of mass m attached to a string moves in a vertical plane under gravity. Unlike horizontal circular motion, the speed varies with height — fastest at the bottom, slowest at the top.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Why speed varies:** gravity does work on the body as it moves vertically. Kinetic energy is converted to gravitational PE on the way up, and back to KE on the way down. Total mechanical energy is conserved (ignoring air resistance).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Critical distinction — string vs rigid rod:** a string can only pull (tension ≥ 0), so the body must have enough speed at the top to keep the string taut. A rigid rod can push or pull, so the body can reach the top with zero speed.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Minimum speed at the top (string):** at the top, both tension T and weight mg point downward: T + mg = mv²/r. The minimum speed occurs when T → 0: mg = mv_min²/r → v_top(min) = √(gr). Below this, the string goes slack.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Minimum speed at the bottom (string):** using energy conservation from bottom to top: ½mv_bottom² = ½mv_top² + mg(2r). With v_top = √(gr): ½v_bottom² = ½(gr) + 2gr → v_bottom² = 5gr → v_bottom(min) = √(5gr). This is the critical NEB result.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Minimum speed at the bottom (rod):** since the rod can push, the body can reach the top with v_top = 0. Energy conservation: ½v_bottom² = 0 + 2gr → v_bottom(min) = √(2gr). This is lower than the string case.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Tension at the bottom:** T_bottom − mg = mv_bottom²/r → T_bottom = mg + mv_bottom²/r. For the minimum case (string): T_bottom = mg + 5mg = 6mg. The tension at the bottom is 6 times the weight.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Tension at the top:** T_top + mg = mv_top²/r → T_top = mv_top²/r − mg. For the minimum case (string): T_top = 0.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Worked example:** a 0.5 kg bob on a 1.5 m string. v_top(min) = √(9.8 × 1.5) = 3.83 m/s. v_bottom(min) = √(5 × 9.8 × 1.5) = 8.57 m/s. T_bottom = 6 × 0.5 × 9.8 = 29.4 N.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** deriving v_top(min) = √(gr) and v_bottom(min) = √(5gr); comparing string vs rod; finding tension at top and bottom; energy conservation between positions.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Energy diagram for vertical circle:** plot KE, PE, and total E vs. height. KE is maximum at the bottom (½mv_b²), minimum at the top (½mv_t²). PE is minimum (0) at the bottom and maximum (mg·2r) at the top. Total energy E = ½mv_b² = ½mv_t² + 2mgr — a horizontal line on the diagram.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Tension variation around the circle:** at any angle θ from the bottom, T = mv²/r + mg cos θ. At the bottom (θ = 0°): T = mg + mv²/r (maximum). At the top (θ = 180°): T = mv²/r − mg (minimum). The tension difference between bottom and top is always 6mg when the minimum-speed condition holds.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Worked example — tension at arbitrary point:** a 0.2 kg bob on a 0.8 m string has speed 6 m/s at the bottom. At the top: ½v_t² = ½ × 36 − 9.8 × 1.6 = 18 − 15.68 = 2.32 → v_t = 2.15 m/s. T_top = 0.2 × 4.62/0.8 − 1.96 = 1.16 − 1.96 = −0.80 N. Since T < 0, the string goes slack before reaching the top. The bob cannot complete the circle.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Rod vs. string — key differences:** a rod can support compression, so the bob can reach the top with v = 0. The rod pushes upward with force mg at the top. Minimum bottom speed for a rod: √(2gr). For a string: √(5gr). The factor 5 versus 2 is the critical distinction tested in NEB.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Complete-the-circle condition:** for a string, the condition is v_bottom ≥ √(5gr). For a rod, it is v_bottom ≥ √(2gr). If v_bottom is between √(2gr) and √(5gr), the bob completes the circle with a rod but not with a string.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>The minimum speed at the top (string) is √(gr), independent of mass.</td></tr><tr><td>The minimum speed at the bottom (string) is √(5gr) — the most tested result.</td></tr><tr><td>For a rigid rod, v_bottom(min) = √(2gr) — the rod can push, not just pull.</td></tr><tr><td>Tension at the bottom is always maximum; at the top it is minimum.</td></tr><tr><td>At the minimum case (string), T_bottom = 6mg — six times the weight.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The speed is constant in vertical circular motion.' ✅ The speed VARIES — gravity speeds up the body on the way down and slows it on the way up. Only energy is conserved.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The minimum speed at the top is zero.' ✅ For a STRING, the minimum speed at the top is √(gr). For a ROD, it is indeed zero.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The tension is the same at all points.' ✅ The tension varies — maximum at the bottom (T = mg + mv²/r) and minimum at the top (T = mv²/r − mg).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The minimum speed at the bottom is √(2gr).' ✅ That is for a ROD. For a STRING, it is √(5gr) — the body must have enough energy to reach the top with speed √(gr).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'If the string goes slack, the body stops.' ✅ If the string goes slack, the body becomes a projectile — it follows a parabolic path under gravity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The centripetal force is constant.' ✅ The centripetal force (mv²/r) varies because v varies. At the bottom, it is maximum; at the top, it is minimum.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Motion in a vertical circle involves varying speed due to gravity. For a string, the minimum speed at the top is √(gr) and at the bottom is √(5gr). A rigid rod allows v_top = 0, giving v_bottom(min) = √(2gr). Tension is maximum at the bottom (T = mg + mv²/r) and minimum at the top. Energy conservation links speeds at different heights.</p><hr><h2>Applications of Banking</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Banking of roads:** the practice of raising the outer edge of a curved road above the inner edge so that the normal reaction from the road surface provides part or all of the centripetal force needed to turn. This reduces reliance on friction and prevents skidding.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Why banking is needed:** on a flat road, the entire centripetal force must come from friction: F_c = μmg. At high speeds or on slippery roads, friction may be insufficient and the vehicle skids outward. Banking tilts the road so the normal force has a horizontal component pointing toward the center.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Forces on a banked road:** the road is tilted at angle θ. The normal reaction N acts perpendicular to the road surface. Resolving N: vertical component N cos θ balances weight (N cos θ = mg); horizontal component N sin θ provides centripetal force (N sin θ = mv²/r).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Key equation — ideal banking angle (no friction needed):** dividing the two equations: tan θ = v²/(rg). For a given radius r and design speed v, the road should be banked at angle θ = arctan(v²/(rg)). At this angle, no friction is required.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Friction on a banked road:** if the vehicle moves faster than the design speed, friction acts DOWN the slope (preventing outward skid). If slower, friction acts UP the slope (preventing inward sliding). The general formula: tan(θ + φ) = v²/(rg), where φ = arctan(μ).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Maximum speed on a banked road with friction:** v_max = √[rg(tan θ + μ)/(1 − μ tan θ)]. When μ = 0 (no friction), this reduces to v = √(rg tan θ). When θ = 0 (flat road), it reduces to v = √(μrg).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Worked example — car on banked curve:** a road of radius 100 m is banked at θ = 15°. Design speed (no friction): v = √(100 × 9.8 × tan 15°) = √(100 × 9.8 × 0.268) = √262.6 = 16.2 m/s (≈ 58 km/h).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Worked example — with friction:** same road with μ = 0.3. v_max = √[100 × 9.8 × (0.268 + 0.3)/(1 − 0.3 × 0.268)] = √[980 × 0.568 / 0.92] = √605 = 24.6 m/s (≈ 89 km/h).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Railway banking (superelevation):** railways use the same principle. The outer rail is raised by height h above the inner rail. For track gauge w and banking angle θ: tan θ ≈ h/w. This prevents wear on rails and ensures passenger comfort.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** deriving tan θ = v²/(rg); finding the design speed for a given banking angle; maximum speed with friction; railway superelevation problems.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Force resolution on a banked road — detailed derivation:** draw the FBD with N perpendicular to the road and mg vertically down. Resolve N into: vertical component N cos θ (balances weight) and horizontal component N sin θ (provides centripetal force). From N cos θ = mg: N = mg/cos θ. From N sin θ = mv²/r: (mg/cos θ) sin θ = mv²/r → mg tan θ = mv²/r → tan θ = v²/(rg). This derivation is the standard NEB proof.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Friction on a banked road — general case:** when friction is present, there are two limiting cases. Case 1 (going too fast): friction acts DOWN the slope, adding to the centripetal force. Case 2 (going too slow): friction acts UP the slope, preventing inward sliding. The general equation combining both: N sin θ ± f cos θ = mv²/r (horizontal) and N cos θ ∓ f sin θ = mg (vertical), where f = μN.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Worked example — finding banking angle:** a curve of radius 150 m is designed for 90 km/h (25 m/s). Required banking angle: tan θ = v²/(rg) = 625/(150 × 9.8) = 0.425 → θ = 23.0°. At this angle, a car can negotiate the curve without any friction.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Worked example — height of outer rail:** a railway track of gauge 1.5 m is banked at 5°. The height by which the outer rail is raised: h = w tan θ = 1.5 × tan 5° = 1.5 × 0.0875 = 0.131 m ≈ 13.1 cm. This is a typical NEB numerical problem.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Why banking reduces tire wear:** on a flat road, friction must provide all centripetal force, causing lateral tire stress. On a banked road at the design speed, the normal force provides the centripetal component — no lateral friction needed. This dramatically reduces tire wear and fuel consumption.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>The ideal banking angle tan θ = v²/(rg) is independent of vehicle mass.</td></tr><tr><td>At design speed, no friction is needed — the horizontal component of normal force provides all centripetal force.</td></tr><tr><td>Banking with friction increases the maximum safe speed beyond the design speed.</td></tr><tr><td>Railway superelevation h = w tan θ applies the same physics to train tracks.</td></tr><tr><td>Over-banking (θ too large for the speed) causes inward sliding; under-banking causes outward skidding.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Banking eliminates friction entirely.' ✅ Banking REDUCES friction requirement. At the design speed (tan θ = v²/(rg)), friction is zero. At other speeds, friction is still needed.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A steeper bank angle is always better.' ✅ Too steep a bank angle causes problems at low speeds — the vehicle may slide inward. The ideal angle depends on the design speed.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The normal force equals mg on a banked road.' ✅ The normal force is N = mg/cos θ, which is GREATER than mg because the road is tilted.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Banking angle depends on the mass of the vehicle.' ✅ The ideal banking angle tan θ = v²/(rg) is independent of mass — it depends only on speed and radius.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'On a banked road, friction always acts up the slope.' ✅ Friction direction depends on speed: if v > design speed, friction acts DOWN the slope; if v < design speed, friction acts UP.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A higher banking angle means a lower maximum speed.' ✅ A HIGHER banking angle INCREASES the maximum speed — more of the normal force contributes to centripetal force.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Banking of roads tilts the road surface so the normal force provides part or all of the centripetal force. The ideal angle (no friction needed) is tan θ = v²/(rg), independent of mass. With friction, the maximum speed is v_max = √[rg(tan θ + μ)/(1 − μ tan θ)]. Railway tracks use superelevation h = w tan θ. Banking prevents skidding and reduces tire wear.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Angular Displacement, Velocity and Acceleration</td><td>Angular displacement (θ), angular velocity (ω = dθ/dt), and angular acceleration (α = dω/dt) describe rotational motion. For constant angular acceleration, the kinematic equations mirror linear motion. The links v = rω and a_c = rω² connect rotational and translational quantities. rpm is converted to rad/s by multiplying by 2π/60.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Angular Displacement, Velocity and Acceleration</td><td>Angular displacement (θ), angular velocity (ω = dθ/dt), and angular acceleration (α = dω/dt) describe rotational motion. For constant angular acceleration, the kinematic equations mirror linear motion. The links v = rω and a_c = rω² connect rotational and translational quantities. rpm is converted to rad/s by multiplying by 2π/60. Angular displacement, velocity and acceleration is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Centripetal Acceleration and Force</td><td>Centripetal acceleration (a_c = v²/r = rω²) is directed toward the centre of a circular path. The centripetal force (F_c = mv²/r) is the resultant of real forces (tension, gravity, friction) — not a new force. Maximum-speed problems (string breaking, car skidding) and identifying which real force provides F_c are the key NEB topics.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Relation Between Angular and Linear Velocity and Acceleration</td><td>Angular displacement (θ), angular velocity (ω = dθ/dt), and angular acceleration (α = dω/dt) describe rotational motion. For constant angular acceleration, the kinematic equations mirror linear motion. The links v = rω and a_c = rω² connect rotational and translational quantities. rpm is converted to rad/s by multiplying by 2π/60. Relation between angular and linear velocity and acceleration is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Centripetal Acceleration and Centripetal Force</td><td>Centripetal acceleration (a_c = v²/r = rω²) is directed toward the centre of a circular path. The centripetal force (F_c = mv²/r) is the resultant of real forces (tension, gravity, friction) — not a new force. Maximum-speed problems (string breaking, car skidding) and identifying which real force provides F_c are the key NEB topics.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Conical Pendulum</td><td>A conical pendulum consists of a bob moving in a horizontal circle with the string tracing a cone. The key equations are T cos θ = mg and T sin θ = mω²L sin θ, giving ω = √(g/L cos θ) and T_period = 2π√(L cos θ/g). The period is independent of the bob's mass and depends only on the vertical height of the cone. A conical pendulum consists of a bob moving in a horizontal circle with the string tracing a cone. The key equations are $T \cos\theta = mg$ and $T \sin\theta = m\omega^2 L \sin\theta$, giving $\omega = \sqrt{\frac{g}{L \cos\theta}}$ and $T_{period} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L \cos\theta}{g}}$. The period is independent of the bob's mass and depends only on the vertical height of the cone. Conical pendulum is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Motion in a Vertical Circle</td><td>Motion in a vertical circle involves varying speed due to gravity. For a string, the minimum speed at the top is √(gr) and at the bottom is √(5gr). A rigid rod allows v_top = 0, giving v_bottom(min) = √(2gr). Tension is maximum at the bottom (T = mg + mv²/r) and minimum at the top. Energy conservation links speeds at different heights.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Applications of Banking</td><td>Banking of roads tilts the road surface so the normal force provides part or all of the centripetal force. The ideal angle (no friction needed) is tan θ = v²/(rg), independent of mass. With friction, the maximum speed is v_max = √[rg(tan θ + μ)/(1 − μ tan θ)]. Railway tracks use superelevation h = w tan θ. Banking prevents skidding and reduces tire wear.</td></tr><tr><td>9: Motion in a Vertical Circle</td><td>Motion in a vertical circle involves varying speed due to gravity. For a string, the minimum speed at the top is √(gr) and at the bottom is √(5gr). A rigid rod allows v_top = 0, giving v_bottom(min) = √(2gr). Tension is maximum at the bottom (T = mg + mv²/r) and minimum at the top. Energy conservation links speeds at different heights.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Applications of Banking</td><td>Banking of roads tilts the road surface so the normal force provides part or all of the centripetal force. The ideal angle (no friction needed) is tan θ = v²/(rg), independent of mass. With friction, the maximum speed is v_max = √[rg(tan θ + μ)/(1 − μ tan θ)]. Railway tracks use superelevation h = w tan θ. Banking prevents skidding and reduces tire wear.</td></tr></table></body></html>